5 Compare with Direct Estimates
Direct estimates provide a useful diagnostic reference. They are noisy for small domains, but they show the pattern in the observed survey data before model-based smoothing and poststratification.


Code
direct_commute &lt;- survey_mrp |&gt;
  group_by(domain_id) |&gt;
  summarise(
    direct = weighted.mean(indicator, weight, na.rm = TRUE),
    n = n(),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

commute_comparison &lt;- msoa_domains |&gt;
  left_join(direct_commute, by = "domain_id") |&gt;
  left_join(mrp_draws_by_domain, by = "domain_id")

ranked_commute &lt;- commute_comparison |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(direct), !is.na(mrp_mean)) |&gt;
  arrange(direct, domain_id) |&gt;
  mutate(direct_estimate_rank = row_number())

ggplot(ranked_commute, aes(x = direct_estimate_rank)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = mrp_lower, ymax = mrp_upper), fill = "#54A24B", alpha = 0.16) +
  geom_line(aes(y = direct, colour = "Direct"), linewidth = 0.45) +
  geom_line(aes(y = mrp_mean, colour = "MRP"), linewidth = 0.45) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(
    "Direct" = "blue",
    "MRP" = "#54A24B"
  )) +
  labs(
    title = "Direct vs. MRP estimates",
    x = "MSOAs with direct estimates, ranked by direct estimate",
    y = "Mean commute distance (miles)",
    colour = NULL
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

2 Visualisation guidelines
Useful visual outputs include:

estimate maps;
uncertainty maps;
direct-versus-modelled estimate plots;
ranked line graphs with uncertainty intervals;
maps of modelled-minus-direct or modelled-minus-benchmark differences;
tables flagging areas with high uncertainty, low sample size, or strong model influence.

Good SAE visualisation should avoid giving a false sense of precision. A map of estimates alone can look authoritative even when many areas have wide intervals. Pairing estimate maps with uncertainty maps, or using line graphs with shaded intervals, helps keep the uncertainty visible.
